<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nellore</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nellore is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Penna river near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> coast. In the 12th and 13th centuries it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-cholas">Telugu Chodas</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatavarman-sundara-pandya-i">Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I</a> performed a victory coronation there. It later came under the Kakatiyas and Vijayanagara and is now a district headquarters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nellore.jpg" alt="Nellore" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: YVSREDDY · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_Ranganathaswamy_Temple,_Galigopuram,_Nellore_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nellore, also spelt Nelluru, stands on the banks of the Penna river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, about 24 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, and is the headquarters of Nellore district. It lies about 170 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a> and 279 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayawada">Vijayawada</a>. The name has several explanations. A story from the Sthala Purana connects it with a sacred lingam found beneath a nelli tree, the emblic myrobalan, while another derives it from the Tamil word nellu, meaning paddy or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, in a region known for rice cultivation. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the area was called Vikrama Simhapuri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nellore was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> of the Mauryan dynasty in the 3rd century BCE. Other powers that held it include the Satavahanas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela</a> of the Chedi dynasty, the Pallavas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> and the Eastern Gangas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>. The Pallavas held it until the 6th century CE, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> ruled it for a long period; Tamil inscriptions show it formed part of their kingdom until their decline in the 13th century. It then passed to the Kakatiyas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and the Arcot Nawabs.</p><p>In 1758 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> under Balwant Rao captured Nellore from Nazibulla. In the 18th century the British took it from the Arcot Nawabs, and it became part of the Madras Presidency. John Boswell produced the first British account in 1873 as collector, covering climate, agriculture, taxation and the leading families, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> and poligars responsible for villages and lands under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>. The British valued Nellore's cultivation and the port of Krishnapatam. Potti Sriramulu, who fasted until death for the formation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, came from Nellore, and in 2008 the district was renamed in his honour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 505,258 people in the city. Its municipality was constituted on 1 November 1866 and became a corporation on 18 October 2004. Vikrama Simhapuri University is in the city. The Rottela Panduga, or Roti festival, is held every year at the Bara Shaheed Dargah on the banks of Swarnala Cheruvu and draws visitors of all faiths. The poet Tikkana, one of the Trinity of Poets, is among those associated with the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nellore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Feudatories such as the Telugu Cholas of Velanati and <b>Nellore</b> held the eastern coast for the Cholas, aided their campaigns and levied tribute on the Kannada kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…I made the Pandyas paramount in the south: he defeated the Chola king Rajendra III, drove the Hoysalas out of the Tamil country and killed the Hoysala king Vira Someshvara c. 1262, overcame the Telugu Chodas of Nellore and fought the Kakatiyas, performing a victory coronation at <b>Nellore</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
